# Issue #5909 — Scrub by Mouse Drag Mishandles Next Video

## ROOT CAUSE
`iina/PlayerCore.swift:952-963` (`PlayerCore.seek(percent:forceExact:)`) — the
clamp `percent.clamped(to: 0..<100)` only protects against the literal value
`100.0`; it produces `100.0.nextDown` ≈ `99.99999999999999%`, which is still
close enough to EOF that mpv's `absolute-percent+exact` seek triggers an
end-of-file in the demuxer and auto-advances the playlist. Combined with
`PlaySliderCell.awakeFromNib` (maxValue=100) and
`PlayerWindowController.updatePlayTime` (`iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:606-633`)
overwriting `playSlider.doubleValue` while the user is still tracking the
mouse, this turns a single drag-past-end into a cascade.

## CONTROL FLOW
1. User presses on the scrubber knob and drags rightward.
   `PlaySliderCell.startTracking` (`iina/PlaySliderCell.swift:181-188`) pauses
   the player and begins continuous mouse tracking. NSSliderCell will keep
   recomputing `doubleValue` from the cursor's X position and emitting the
   action on every change.
2. As the cursor passes the right edge of the slider, `sender.doubleValue` is
   pinned to `maxValue` (100, set in `PlaySliderCell.awakeFromNib`).
   `PlayerWindowController.playSliderChanges`
   (`iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:689-693`) computes
   `percentage = 100 * sender.doubleValue / sender.maxValue == 100` and calls
   `player.seek(percent: 100, forceExact: !followGlobalSeekTypeWhenAdjustSlider)`.
3. `PlayerCore.seek(percent:)` clamps via `percent.clamped(to: 0..<100)`. The
   `FloatingPoint.clamped` extension (`iina/Extensions.swift:413-421`) returns
   `range.upperBound.nextDown` for any input ≥ upperBound — i.e.
   ~`99.99999999999999`. The command sent is
   `seek 99.99999999999999 absolute-percent+exact`.
4. mpv resolves the percent against `info.videoDuration`, lands either a hair
   before the final frame or, for videos with sparse keyframes, at EOF as the
   inline comment in `seek(percent:)` already warns; it then fires
   `MPV_EVENT_END_FILE` and auto-advances to the next playlist entry.
5. On the new file the standard pipeline runs:
   `fileStarted` (`iina/PlayerCore.swift:2000`) →
   `fileLoaded` (`iina/PlayerCore.swift:2087`) →
   `playbackRestarted` (`iina/PlayerCore.swift:2302-2308`) which calls
   `syncUI(.time)`. That dispatches to
   `currentController.updatePlayTime(..., andProgressBar: true)`, which
   executes `playSlider.doubleValue = percentage` with
   `percentage = pos/duration*100 ≈ 0` for the freshly opened file
   (`iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:629-631`).
6. NSSliderCell is *still* in its tracking loop because the user has not
   released the mouse. The cell's next `continueTracking` tick recomputes the
   slider value from the cursor's X position — which is still pinned at the
   right edge — and writes `doubleValue` back to `100`, then emits the action
   message. We are back at step 2.
7. The loop repeats once per file, cascading through the playlist for as long
   as the user keeps the mouse button down past the right edge of the
   scrubber.

## WHY IT CASCADES
The clamp in `seek(percent:)` is effectively cosmetic: `0..<100` excludes only
the exact value `100.0`, and the resulting `nextDown(100)` is indistinguishable
from EOF for any real video (the time delta is on the order of `1e-14 *
duration` seconds, well below mpv's seek precision and any keyframe gap). The
author already knew this — the inline comment on line 956 admits "it still
won't work for videos with large keyframe interval." What turns one accidental
advance into an unbounded cascade is that nothing suppresses the slider's
tracking session across the file change: `updatePlayTime` overwrites
`playSlider.doubleValue` to ~0 after each new file loads, and on the next mouse
tick NSSliderCell snaps the value back to the cursor's still-pinned position
(~100), which fires `playSliderChanges` again. Each iteration of this loop is
a fresh near-100% seek that mpv resolves to EOF on the new file, so the
playlist marches forward one entry per cycle. The drag-time pause performed by
`PlaySliderCell.startTracking` does not save us — the seeks are explicit
commands, not natural playback reaching EOF.

## SUGGESTED FIX
Make the clamp actually safe by clamping in the time domain rather than the
percent domain. Inside `PlayerCore.seek(percent:forceExact:)`, when
`info.videoDuration` is known, convert to seconds, clamp to
`max(0, duration - safetyMargin)` (e.g., `duration - 1.0` second), and dispatch
`seek <seconds> absolute+exact` instead of
`seek <percent> absolute-percent+exact`. That keeps the seek strictly inside
the file regardless of keyframe layout and removes the EOF trigger that drives
the cascade — the slider drag will then plateau at "near end of current file"
the way the user expects, and the playlist will only advance when the file is
actually allowed to finish (or when the user releases the scrubber). A
complementary belt-and-braces change is to short-circuit
`PlayerWindowController.updatePlayTime`'s `playSlider.doubleValue = percentage`
assignment while the slider's cell `isHighlighted` (i.e., while the user is
dragging), so that even if a future regression reintroduces an EOF-triggering
seek, the tracking session does not get re-armed by the controller stomping on
the slider value.
